<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">company train</ansi>

Companions can learn optional skills, so you shape each member. Cooking
and Scribe are the optional skills as the world ships. Class skills stay
automatic: a companion's archetype and level give it its specialist
skills, such as a ranger's Read the Trail (<ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>),
and its stats (<ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>). Neither is trained.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Training points</ansi>

A companion earns training points as it levels, at the same rate you
do: 1 a level as the world ships, so a level-6 companion has earned 6.
A rank costs its number in points, as yours do at a trainer: rank 1
costs 1, rank 2 costs 2, rank 3 costs 3 and rank 4 costs 4. Ranks 1 and
2 together cost 3, so that level-6 companion has 3 left.

Points are never stored. They are worked out each time from the
companion's level, less what its ranks cost, so dying, being raised,
logging out or a restart never gives any back. A level lost to death
keeps every rank; the companion owes the points instead (shown as 0,
with what is owed), and its next levels repay them before it can train
again. A recruit who arrived already knowing a skill paid nothing for
those ranks.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where</ansi>

  Ranks 1 and 2: at your own camp (the fire need not be lit) or from a
  trainer who teaches that rank.
  Ranks 3 and 4: only from a trainer who teaches that rank. The Waymark
  Inn in Dunmar teaches Cooking up to rank 4.

The companion must be alive, with you, and not fighting. Nobody trains
during a battle, a journey, or a rest.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company train</ansi>
  Every companion's training points, its optional skills and ranks,
  and what the next rank costs.

  <ansi fg="command">company train [member]</ansi>
  One companion's choices.

  <ansi fg="command">company train [member] [skill]</ansi>
  A preview: the rank, where, the cost, the points left after, and what
  the skill does. It changes nothing.

  <ansi fg="command">company train [member] [skill] [rank] confirm</ansi>
  Train it. The preview shows the exact line to type, with the rank, and
  typing that line twice trains nothing more: the companion already has
  that rank. Without a rank, confirm trains the next one.

  <ansi fg="command">company inspect [member]</ansi>
  A companion's level, optional skills, and training points.

Name a member by number (<ansi fg="command">#2</ansi>) or by name. <ansi fg="command">experience</ansi> shows any
points a companion has left, and the web client's Character > Skills
lists every companion's skills and points.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What a trained skill does</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Cooking</ansi>  The best cook with you cooks at camp (<ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>).
  <ansi fg="yellow">Scribe</ansi>   Wizards and clerics only. The best Scribe with you reads
           rare gear at camp and in the field (<ansi fg="command">help scribe</ansi>).

Your own skills are trained with <ansi fg="command">train</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help train</ansi>).
See also: <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>.
